// @test: differential
// @tier: 0,1
// @level: L0
// @tags: aot, codegen, reference, return, slot-address, regression
// @timeout: 30000
// @expected-exit: 0
// @expected-stdout-begin
// field=u
// len=1
// sum=7
// onward=42
// mutated=42
// @expected-stdout-end
//
// Regression guard for T1188 / T1192: a `-> &T` accessor whose body is
// `&self.field` hands the caller the ADDRESS of a record slot, and the
// CALLER owes the load.
//
// The RefField arm yields that address on purpose — T1056 established
// that returning the loaded value made `*guard` and `fn get(&self) ->
// &Int` fault on the number itself. What was missing is the other half:
// no caller loaded through it, so every consumer applied its own layout
// to an address. One defect wearing four faces, chosen by whatever the
// neighbouring bytes happened to be — measured against the interpreter,
// which was right every time:
//
//     shared.deref().url            u      ->  (empty)
//     bag.borrow().len()            1      ->  0
//     for x in bag.borrow().iter()  7      ->  0
//     path.as_str() -> as_bytes()   /tmp   ->  SIGSEGV at 0x0
//
// The last of those is T1192: `copy_path_nul` read a Text handle that
// was really a stack address and dereferenced NULL, which is why the
// registry died after 29 scenes.
//
// The fix asks ONE walk of both bodies — the callee's says whether a
// slot address is handed back, the caller's says whether it is wanted as
// an address here — and loads only when the caller wants contents.
//
// The last two lines are the poles that must NOT move: `onward` returns
// the reference to its own caller and `mutated` writes through it, so
// both need the address to survive. A fix that loaded unconditionally
// passes the first three lines and breaks these two.
//
// THE THIRD SURVIVAL POLE IS MISSING ON PURPOSE, and the reason is worth
// carrying: `*map.entry(k).or_insert_with(f) += 1` is the shape that
// nearly shipped broken, but it cannot be a pole here because
// `Map.entry` NULL-dereferences in the TIER-0 INTERPRETER (opcode 0x62,
// `Null pointer dereference at Map.entry`), so a differential spec on it
// would fail for a reason that is not this defect. Filed separately.
//
// `MapEntry.or_insert_with` returns `&mut V` by forwarding
// `OccupiedEntry.get_mut`, and its body writes Reg(2) on BOTH match
// arms:
//
//     6: Call { dst: Reg(5), ... }     entry.get_mut()   seed {5}
//     7: Mov  { dst: 2, src: 5 }       Occupied arm      alias {2,5}
//    17: Mov  { dst: 2, src: 6 }       Vacant arm — KILLED 2
//    20: Ret  { value: 8 }             8 came from 2, never seen
//
// Two writes to one register on MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE arms, read by a
// LINEAR walk as consecutive. The walk answered "this value is dropped
// here" about a value the function RETURNS, which would have made
// `*e += 1` write to a stack temp instead of the map — no crash, no
// diagnostic, a map that silently stops counting. That is a property of
// reading VBC in program order without following branches, and the fix
// is that this site's alias set never kills, so every wrong answer
// degrades to "do not load".

mount core.prelude.*;

// A NOTE ON THE METHOD NAMES, so nobody tidies them: `lend_items`,
// `nth_ref`, `only_field`, `forwarded`, `writable_n` and `lend_inner`
// are deliberately names `core/` does not hold. The obvious spellings —
// `get`, `at`, `slot`, `peek`, `borrow` — are all stdlib names, and a
// user function can lose its bare-name slot to a stdlib namesake of a
// different arity (T1191: `count(&l)` in a five-line program ran
// `core.database.sqlite.native.eqp_node_emitter.node.count`). This spec
// must be able to fail for exactly one reason.


type Rec is { url: Text };
type Bag is { items: List<Int> };
type Inner is { v: Int };
type Holder is { inner: Inner };
type Cell is { n: Int };

implement Bag {
    fn lend_items(&self) -> &List<Int> { &self.items }
}

implement Inner {
    fn only_field(&self) -> &Int { &self.v }
}

implement Holder {
    // Forwards someone else's returned reference — the address must
    // still be an address when it reaches `main`.
    fn forwarded(&self) -> &Int { self.inner.only_field() }
}

implement Cell {
    fn writable_n(&mut self) -> &mut Int { &mut self.n }
}

type Wrap is { inner: Rec };
implement Wrap {
    fn lend_inner(&self) -> &Rec { &self.inner }
}

fn main() -> Int {
    // A field read through a returned reference.
    let w = Wrap { inner: Rec { url: "u" } };
    print(f"field={w.lend_inner().url}");

    // A container method on a returned reference: the length site
    // decides cell-vs-object by comparing the first word against
    // 4294967295, so a slot address makes the branch data-dependent.
    let mut l: List<Int> = List.new();
    l.push(7);
    let b = Bag { items: l };
    print(f"len={b.lend_items().len()}");

    // The same reference, ITERATED.
    let mut total = 0;
    for x in b.lend_items().iter() { total = total + x; }
    print(f"sum={total}");

    // The address must SURVIVE: returned onward, then dereferenced.
    let h = Holder { inner: Inner { v: 42 } };
    print(f"onward={*h.forwarded()}");

    // The address must SURVIVE: written through.
    let mut c = Cell { n: 1 };
    let r = c.writable_n();
    *r = 42;
    print(f"mutated={c.n}");

    0
}
